<Project>

  <!--
    Repo-wide settings that depend on properties the PROJECT sets.

    This file exists because of an ordering rule that is easy to get wrong:
    Directory.Build.props is imported at the TOP of every csproj, before the
    project's own <PropertyGroup> runs, so a condition there on a project-defined
    property like VstPortable is always evaluated against an empty value and
    silently does nothing. Directory.Build.targets is imported at the BOTTOM,
    after the project has set its properties, which is where this belongs.

    The failure mode was not a build error — it was CA1416 quietly staying a
    warning while the props file appeared to promote it. Verified by adding a
    Registry call to Core and confirming the build now fails.
  -->

  <!--
    Strictness for the platform-neutral half of the tree. A project opts in with
    <VstPortable>true</VstPortable> to declare "I must compile and run on both
    Windows and Linux", which turns CA1416 (platform compatibility) into a build
    error. A stray `using Microsoft.Win32;` then fails on the machine that wrote
    it rather than on the other platform's CI job days later.

    This is the mechanical form of R-12 in docs/LINUX-PORT-PLAN.md: Windows-only
    machinery cannot follow us into shared code, because shared code that touches
    it does not compile.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(VstPortable)' == 'true'">
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);CA1416</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    THE 330 MB THAT MUST NOT SHIP — Phase 8b.

    VibeSuperTonic.Onnx.Ort links Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime.Gpu.Linux for one
    reason: its libonnxruntime.so exports the CUDA entry point and the CPU
    package's does not (measured — the mixture fails with
    EntryPointNotFoundException). What comes with it is
    libonnxruntime_providers_cuda.so at 330 MB, against a 51 MB archive, and it
    is useless without the CUDA and cuDNN that build/install-gpu.sh fetches
    alongside it. So the pack is opt-in and these two files are removed.

    REPO-WIDE RATHER THAN IN THE BACKEND CSPROJ, and that is the whole reason
    this lives here: a target inside Onnx.Ort governs Onnx.Ort's own output, not
    the publish of the daemon that references it. The first version of this was
    in the csproj, it looked correct, `dotnet build` was clean — and
    build/pack-tar.sh caught 330 MB sitting in the composed tree. Assertion 4
    there is the guard that fires if this stops working.

    Removed from the publish list rather than from the package reference,
    because the reference is what puts the working libonnxruntime.so in place.
  -->
  <Target Name="VstRemoveOptionalGpuProviders" AfterTargets="ComputeResolvedFilesToPublishList">
    <ItemGroup>
      <ResolvedFileToPublish
        Remove="@(ResolvedFileToPublish)"
        Condition="'%(Filename)' == 'libonnxruntime_providers_cuda'
                or '%(Filename)' == 'libonnxruntime_providers_tensorrt'" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

</Project>
